### 4.5 Warmup window - drop cold-cache cycles from the report

Every cycle figure above (marker / pc / icount / simpoint) treats the entire
vsim window as the measured region. The first few thousand vsim insns hit
cold caches/BPU/TLB and run slower than steady state; if you only care
about the warm-cache cost, those cycles are noise. Pass
`--vsim-warmup-icount K` to record a measurement-start snapshot K committed
insns after vsim entry, splitting the window into a cold prefix and a warm
tail.

Mechanism: the snapshot stays inside one vsim process so caches do not
get flushed - only the recorded boundary moves. K=0 (default) reproduces the
no-warmup behaviour bit-for-bit. The K-th-commit boundary is captured at
the moment the counter trips, then vsim resumes; in marker / PC mode the
snapshot lands on exactly the K-th retire.

```sh
./andesim run tests/fixtures/rt_c_v_matmul.elf \
  --mode hybrid --trigger marker --engine vsim:ax45mpv_premium --vsim-warmup-icount 1000
```

The snapshot - the (`mcycle`, `minstret`) pair at the K-th commit - is
written into the drained state file (both values stay zero when K=0 or
the workload exited inside the warmup window). `./andesim run` still
prints the single `[<kind> window_cycles] N` line for the **whole**
window; to get the warm-tail figure, subtract the recorded snapshot from
the drained `mcycle`. The marker/pc e2e harness surfaces the snapshot
directly as a `[<fixture> warmup] K=... mcycle_at_measure_start=...
minstret_at_measure_start=...` line.

Coming from gem5: this is the analogue of `--warmup-insts N` followed by
an implicit `m5.stats.reset()`. The constraint differs - QEMU has no
cache model, so unlike gem5 the warmup MUST happen inside the cycle-
accurate phase (vsim). Otherwise the contract is identical: K cold
insns, then the measured window.

### 4.6 Capturing a Kanata pipeline trace of the ROI

When debugging a hot path on vsim, pass `--kanata FILE` to `./andesim run`
in marker or pc mode. The resulting trace contains pipeline records only
for the ROI region (between QEMU 1's drain and vsim's drain) -- vsim
boot and DMI resume overhead are filtered out.

```sh
# marker mode: the ROI is the span between the two ROI markers (slti x0,x0,0x5A0/1)
# markers already in the source. vsim auto-derives the window.
./andesim run tests/fixtures/rt_c_v_matmul.elf \
  --mode hybrid --trigger marker --engine vsim:ax45mpv_premium --kanata /tmp/roi.kanata

# pc mode: the ROI bounds are whatever PCs you passed.
./andesim run tests/fixtures/rt_c_v_matmul_free.elf \
  --mode hybrid --trigger pc --engine vsim:ax45mpv_premium --pc-start vmatmul_kernel --pc-end 0x1002cc \
  --kanata /tmp/roi.kanata
```

Add `--objdump FILE` so the kanata `L` records embed the disassembly
text next to each PC. Generate the dump once with the toolchain
`objdump -d`:

```sh
$HYBRID_TOOLCHAIN/bin/riscv64-unknown-elf-objdump -d \
  tests/fixtures/rt_c_v_matmul.elf > /tmp/roi.S
./andesim run tests/fixtures/rt_c_v_matmul.elf \
  --mode hybrid --trigger marker --engine vsim:ax45mpv_premium --kanata /tmp/roi.kanata --objdump /tmp/roi.S
```

Open `/tmp/roi.kanata` in [Konata](https://github.com/shioyadan/Konata)
to view the pipeline.

Constraints:

- `--kanata` is only valid with `--mode hybrid --trigger marker` or `--mode hybrid --trigger pc` (the only triggers with a single ROI-exit PC); any other trigger is rejected with rc=2.
- marker mode requires exactly one `slti x0,x0,0x5A1` (exit marker) in the
  ELF. If the binary has more than one, vsim errors out with
  `multiple ... markers in ELF -- use \`--mode hybrid --trigger pc\` with explicit \`--pc-end\``.
  Switch to pc mode and pick the desired exit explicitly.
- `--objdump` requires `--kanata`.

How it works:

- hsim forwards `KANATA_FILE` (and optionally `OBJDUMP_FILE`) to
  `tests/e2e.sh`, which appends `--kanata FILE --kanata-roi`
  (and `--objdump FILE`) to vsim's argv.
- vsim's `--kanata-roi` flag defers the kanata enable until after
  `--resume-from-qemu` loads the input state and `enable_hybrid_handoff`
  scans the ELF, then derives a one-shot `LoopGate`:
  - `start_pc` = the resumed PC (where vsim first fetches user code)
  - `end_pc`   = `--hybrid-exit-pc` (pc mode) or the unique
    `slti x0,x0,0x5A1` PC from CsrTrigger (marker mode)
  - `skip_iters=0`, `record_iters=1` (open on first fetch of start_pc,
    close on first retire of end_pc).

### 4.7 Verifying a hybrid run against a QEMU oracle (`--verify`)

With the QEMU2 handback removed, the code after your ROI never runs, so a
hybrid run normally proves only that a cycle figure came out. `--verify`
(default OFF) cross-checks the run's correctness:

```sh
./andesim run --mode hybrid --trigger marker --engine vsim:ax45mpv_premium --verify your_prog.elf
# ...
# [marker window_cycles] 46  (vsim mcycle=... - qemu mcycle=...)
# [state-diff] PASS: 109 fields match (counters and passthrough CSRs excluded)
# [verify] PASS: vsim drain matches the QEMU oracle
```

How it works: after the normal run, the same ELF re-runs on pure QEMU (the
oracle) with the plugin draining at the ROI exit boundary, sampled at the
exact pc where vsim halted. The pre-ROI path is QEMU in both runs, so the
ROI inputs are identical -- any architectural divergence inside the ROI
(RTL bug, state-restore bug, drain bug) shows up as a field mismatch and
the run exits non-zero, listing each differing field:

```
[state-diff] x10: ref=0x3d got=0x0
[state-diff] FAIL: 1 mismatch(es) (115 fields compared)
```

What is compared: pc, all GPRs, all FPRs (when FP state is live), the V
CSRs and full vector registers (when V state is live), and the writable
M-mode CSRs that round-trip through the handoff -- `mscratch` (L23) and
`mepc`/`mcause`/`mtval`/`mie` (L24), restored at resume and re-read at
drain. What is not:

- `mcycle`/`minstret` -- the engines count differently by design;
- `mip` -- restored and re-read, but its pending bits are hardware-driven
  (the timer bit tracks elapsed time, which differs between QEMU's
  functional clock and vsim's cycle clock), so a compare would
  false-positive;
- `mstatus`, `mtvec` -- passthrough: vsim restores the QEMU1 entry snapshot value; both sides hold the same bits by construction so comparing is trivially true but not useful;
- `satp`, `medeleg`/`mideleg` -- S-mode CSRs, not implemented on the default M-only engine. The privilege level itself IS compared: both
  sides drain the real one (L6), so a wrong-privilege resume shows up as
  a priv mismatch.

Costs and limits: the oracle leg is one extra QEMU run of the whole
program (MHz-class -- cheap next to the vsim leg). `--verify` works with
`--trigger marker|pc`, assumes the single-shot ROI the hybrid run itself
assumes, and requires `--mode hybrid` (the other models have nothing to
cross-check).

### 4.8 Localizing a divergence with checkpoint co-sim (`--cosim`, dev)

`--verify` tells you THAT the vsim leg diverged; `--cosim` tells you
roughly WHERE. It is a developer tool (hidden from the release help, like
`state-diff`): every N retired instructions inside the ROI, the vsim leg
halts via DMI, drains a resumable checkpoint appended to the
`<state>.ckpts` stream (concatenated `hybrid_state_v1` records), and
continues. The driver then re-runs the ELF on pure QEMU with the plugin
draining at the SAME retire counts, and diffs checkpoint k against
checkpoint k (same compare policy as `state-diff`):

```sh
ANDESIM_DEV=1 ./andesim run --mode hybrid --trigger marker \
    --engine vsim:ax45mpv_premium --cosim --cosim-interval 500 my.elf
# ...
# [cosim] PASS: 3 checkpoint(s) + final state match the QEMU oracle (interval 500, 1692 ROI retires)
```

On a divergence, the first mismatching checkpoint brackets the bug:

```
[state-diff] x12: ref=0x2a got=0x2b
[cosim] FAIL: divergence in retires 500..1000 after entry (500 insn window)
        at retire ~1000: pc vsim=0x... qemu=0x...
```

Re-run with a smaller `--cosim-interval` to shrink the window.

Alignment is by retire count, not pc: vsim restores `minstret` from the
entry state, so `checkpoint.minstret - entry.minstret` is the exact number
of post-entry retires (vsim's DMI halt overshoots a few instructions; the
overshoot is simply part of the measured delta, and the oracle counts to
the same number). In pc-trigger mode the boundary instruction itself
retires inside the window and is counted as retire #1 on both sides.

Costs and limits:
- `--trigger marker|pc` only, `--mode hybrid` only, exclusive with
  `--verify` (the final-state check is included).
- No cycle figure: each checkpoint costs a DMI halt + drain + resume on
  the vsim leg, which pollutes the vsim `mcycle` delta, so `--cosim`
  suppresses the `window_cycles` line entirely. Re-run without `--cosim`
  to measure.
- Divergence is localized to an N-instruction window, not an exact
  instruction; intervals comparable to the halt overshoot (tens of
  instructions) are not meaningful.
- Memory is NOT compared -- only the architectural register state at each
  checkpoint (a memory bug surfaces indirectly, when a wrong value is
  loaded into a register).

### 4.9 Lock-step DV co-sim (`--mode cycle --cosim`, dev)

The hybrid `--cosim` above localizes to an N-instruction *window*.
`--mode cycle --cosim` localizes to the *exact instruction*: it runs the
ELF twice from reset -- once on a QEMU oracle, once on the cycle-accurate
DUT -- and drains the full architectural state BEFORE every execution
attempt, so the two record streams align attempt-by-attempt. The first
record that differs is the instruction the DUT got wrong. This is the
lock-step tandem-simulation flow for constraint-generated DV testbenches.

```sh
ANDESIM_DEV=1 ./andesim run --mode cycle --cosim \
    --engine vsim:ax45mpv_premium --pc-start roi_start my_tb.elf
# [cosim] PASS: 184 records match (lock-step, 1 attempt/record)
```

On a divergence it names the exact attempt and both PCs:

```
[cosim] FAIL: first divergence at attempt 41 (after 40 matching)
        pc  vsim=0x100abc  qemu=0x100abc   (vec_madd+0x1c)
        x14      vsim=0xdeadbeef  qemu=0xdeadbeee
```

How it runs: the QEMU oracle goes first (MHz-fast) and defines the record
count K; the DUT is then bounded to K so a diverged hart cannot run away.
The DUT side single-steps over the Debug Module (`dcsr.step`), so each
record boundary is exact -- no halt-slack overshoot. Traps are in scope:
an `ecall`/exception aligns by attempt index on both sides (the handler
entry is the next record), and `mepc`/`mcause` are compared so a
mishandled trap diverges loudly.

The arm pc (`--pc-start <sym|0x>`, default the ELF entry) is where
recording begins. The workload must establish a deterministic
architectural state by then: QEMU seeds the boot-argument registers
(`a0`/`a1`/...) at reset while the RTL resets GPRs to 0, so a workload
that reads those reset values diverges at attempt 0. A proper DV
testbench initializes all its registers in a prologue and arms after it
(see `tests/fixtures/rt_trap_ecall.S`).

Costs and limits:
- `--mode cycle` only; `--cosim-max-records <K>` bounds the run
  (default: run to program exit). Each DUT record is a DMI step + full
  drain -- expect a few records per second, so cap large testbenches.
- Counters (`mcycle`/`minstret`) are NOT compared: on QEMU's `andes_ae350`
  they alias a host free-running timer, not the architectural retire
  count. Records carry them for inspection only.
- `mtval` and `mstatus` are captured but NOT gated (implementation-defined
  bits would false-positive across QEMU vs RTL); `mepc`/`mcause` are.
- Memory is NOT compared (same as the hybrid `--cosim`).
- Asynchronous interrupts are out of scope (the two engines take them at
  different attempt indices); testbenches must be interrupt-free.
- The engine RTL must implement `dcsr.step`; an M-only/step-pinned core
  aborts with a clear "pick an engine that implements it" error.

### 4.10 Debugging with GDB (`--gdb`, `--gdb-port`)

Add `--gdb` to attach a debugger to a running simulation.

**fast model** -- starts QEMU's built-in GDB stub and pauses at the entry point:
```sh
./andesim run --mode fast --gdb my.elf
# In another terminal:
gdb-multiarch -ex 'target remote :1234' my.elf
```
Default port: 1234. Override with `--gdb-port PORT`.

**cycle model** -- resets and **halts** the hart (like QEMU's `-S`), opens vsim's
JTAG bitbang port in the background, waits for it to come up, then execs `$ICEMAN`
-- your JTAG debug server -- in the foreground:
```sh
export ICEMAN=/path/to/jtag-debug-server      # see note
./andesim run --mode cycle --gdb --engine vsim:ax45mpv_premium my.elf
# then point GDB at the server's gdb port (OpenOCD's default is 1111):
gdb-multiarch -ex 'target remote :1111' my.elf
```
`--gdb-port PORT` sets vsim's JTAG bitbang port (default `9104`); GDB itself
connects to the **server's own** gdb port, not this one. The hart starts halted,
so the debugger attaches to a stopped core -- set breakpoints, then `continue`.

`$ICEMAN` is whichever JTAG debug server you point it at; it must speak the
OpenOCD `remote_bitbang` protocol to vsim's port. A current **OpenOCD (0.12+)**
works -- a one-line `$ICEMAN` wrapper can just `exec openocd -f <cfg>`, where
`<cfg>` selects `adapter driver remote_bitbang` at `remote_bitbang port 9104`,
a generic `riscv` target, and a `gdb_port`. A full worked example is in
`docs/andes_sim_demo.ipynb` ("7. Debug with GDB"). **Note:** Andes ICEman's
*own* bundled OpenOCD 0.10 has a `remote_bitbang` bug that crashes against vsim's
DTM, so point `$ICEMAN` at a modern OpenOCD rather than the bundled one.

`--gdb` is not supported with `--mode hybrid` (the handoff pipeline cannot pause for interactive debugging).

